review_comments.body:
A Redis outage during the jti check still turns into 401 invalid_client, and #7028 needs it to stay 500. Here's why: fosite throws away any error from `ClientAssertionJWTValid` and returns a fresh `ErrJTIKnown` (`client_authentication.go:159` in v0.49.0). In `RedisStorage`, that call is where the jti is reserved, so a connection error comes back as jti_known and line 52 maps it to invalid_client. The wrapper can't see the cause, so the store has to pass it on through the request context. Roughly:

In `pkg/authserver/server` (storage already imports this package):
```go
type clientAssertionStoreFailureKey struct{}

type clientAssertionStoreFailure struct{ err error }

// RecordClientAssertionStoreFailure lets a storage backend report that it could
// not check a client assertion's jti for a reason other than a replay. fosite
// discards the error ClientAssertionJWTValid returns, so the client
// authentication strategy reads it from the context instead. Storage
// decorators must pass ctx through to the backend unchanged.
func RecordClientAssertionStoreFailure(ctx context.Context, err error) {
	if f, ok := ctx.Value(clientAssertionStoreFailureKey{}).(*clientAssertionStoreFailure); ok {
		f.err = err
	}
}
```
In the wrapper, before calling `next`:
```go
failure := &clientAssertionStoreFailure{}
ctx = context.WithValue(ctx, clientAssertionStoreFailureKey{}, failure)
client, err := next(ctx, r, form)
if err != nil && failure.err != nil {
	return nil, fosite.ErrServerError.WithWrap(failure.err).WithDebug(failure.err.Error())
}
```
In `RedisStorage.ClientAssertionJWTValid`, in the non-`redis.Nil` branch only:
```go
err = fmt.Errorf("failed to reserve JWT: %w", err)
server.RecordClientAssertionStoreFailure(ctx, err)
return err
```
Don't record the jti-length error. That one is the client's fault, so invalid_client is right for it. `MemoryStorage` has no backend failure, so it doesn't need the call.

Tests:
- In `client_assertion_errors_test.go`, add a case where the stub's `ClientAssertionJWTValid` calls `RecordClientAssertionStoreFailure` and returns an error. It should expect `server_error` with status 500.
- Keep a case where it returns `fosite.ErrJTIKnown` without recording anything. It should still expect 401 invalid_client.
- In `redis_test.go`, add a case that closes the miniredis instance (`mr.Close()`) and calls `ClientAssertionJWTValid` with a context set up by the recorder. It should assert that the error was recorded.

Then update the doc comment above (lines 35-37). As written it says storage failures are returned unchanged, and that's only true once this is in.

Please add a comment here listing what this catches, so the next person doesn't have to dig through fosite:
```suggestion
		// invalid_request: fosite's findPublicKey reports an unknown kid or no
		// matching key this way. It also covers "server configuration does not
		// support OpenID Connect specific authentication methods", which our
		// client types never hit. jti_known: a replayed jti.
		case fosite.ErrInvalidRequest.ErrorField, fosite.ErrJTIKnown.ErrorField:
```

Matching on the message text is fine here, but please say why, so nobody tries to "fix" it:
```suggestion
// clientAssertionClaimErrors are the messages of the claim validation errors
// that fosite returns unwrapped, as plain errors, when it parses a client
// assertion (see fosite/token/jwt MapClaims.Valid). fosite returns the bare
// errors.New value and drops the ValidationError flags, so there is nothing
// typed to match. TestClientAssertionErrorStrategy runs real fosite, so a
// changed message fails that test on upgrade.
```
